 ],
 "literal": "Chief of bards with pot-drums / of the deep mountain pool with wide mouth on Atalai hill / smelling of wild jasmine bundled with water-lilies, your / fragrant forehead, will he forget? Indeed, even though he strives / for rare wealth obstructed by many desert paths, / because it is never fully acquired, delaying is not at all.",
 "translation": "Will he ever forget your fragrant forehead, which smells of wild jasmine bundled with water-lilies from the deep, wide-mouthed mountain pool on Atalai hill, the domain of the chief of bards who play pot-drums? Even though he strives to acquire rare wealth, traversing many obstructing desert paths, since wealth is never fully satisfying, he will not delay his return.",
 "modern": "பதலை என்னும் பானை வடிவப் பறையை முழக்கும் பரிசிலர்களின் தலைவனுடைய அதலை மலையிலுள்ள, அகன்ற வாயைக் கொண்ட ஆழமான சுனையில் பூத்த குவளை மலர்களோடு சேர்த்துத் தொடுத்த காட்டு மல்லிகையின் நறுமணம் வீசும் உன்னுடைய நறிய நெற்றியை அவர் மறந்துவிடுவாரோ? மாட்டார். பல பாலைவழிப் பாதைகள் குறுக்கிடும் அரிய பொருளைத் தேடி அவர் முயன்றாலும், தேடும் பொருள் ஒருபோதும் நிறைவடையாது என்பதால், அவர் அங்கே தங்கித் தன் வருகையை நீட்டிக்கவே மாட்டார்.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the pālai (desert) landscape, where the hero has departed to earn wealth. The heroine is deeply distressed by his absence, fearing he might delay his return. The companion consoles her by reassuring her of the hero's deep love and imminent return. She compares the fragrance of the heroine's forehead to the fresh wild jasmine and water-lilies from the cool pools of Atalai hill, ruled by a generous patron of bards. The companion argues that even though the hero went to seek wealth across difficult desert paths, he knows that wealth is endless and can never be fully satisfying compared to the joy of her company. Therefore, he will not prolong his stay and will return quickly.",
